label("$\theta$", (0.6*A + 0.4*P)/sqrt(2), NW);
label("$\theta$", (0.6*B + 0.4*P)/sqrt(2), NE);
label("$180^\circ - 2 \theta$", (0.6*A + 0.4*B)/sqrt(2), NW);
[/asy]

By the Law of Cosines on triangle $APB,$
\[AB^2 = AP^2 + BP^2 - 2 \cdot AP \cdot BP \cdot \cos (180^\circ - 2 \theta).\]Since $AB$ passes through $F = (1,0),$ $A$ and $B$ satisfy the equation of the ellipse.  Hence,
\[\frac{p^2}{2} + 1 - 2p \cos (180^\circ - 2 \theta) = 1.\]Then $\frac{p^2}{2} = 2p \cos (180^\circ - 2 \theta),$ which simplifies to $p = 4 \cos (180^\circ - 2 \theta) = 4 \cos 2 \theta.$

Since $0 < \theta < 90^\circ,$ $0 < 2 \theta < 180^\circ,$ so $0^\circ < 180^\circ - 2 \theta < 180^\circ.$  Then $0^\circ < \cos (180^\circ - 2 \theta) < 1,$ so $0 < p < 4.$

By symmetry, the points $A$ and $B$ are equidistant from the $y$-axis, which means $p$ is the $x$-coordinate of the midpoint of $AB.$  Since $AB$ passes through $F = (1,0),$ $p = \boxed{2}.$The answer is: 2

